Z||000003||Turbo88||09-19-2001||11:50 PM||||Ok this is a shameless plug <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I am making a turbo kit for the 2.8lt engine. It should be done sometime next month. /plug<P>For an honest comparison from a turbo bias individual, here is my thoughts on a supercharger vs turbocharger. <P>A supercharger uses crank horsepower to spin the compressor. A turbo uses exhaust energy. It costs horsepower to run a supercharger. It costs backpressure to run a turbo. Engine exhuast is very energetic matter. There is heat, momentum (kinetic energy), and pressure. All of this energy is allowed to dissipate inside your exhaust system and right out your tailpipe. A turbo utilizes this energy to spin a turbine and through a shaft, the compressor. As i said before, turbo's under a lot of circumstances add backpressure. This isn't always a bad thing because it helps with the lowend torque (to a certain extent) and at higher RPM it is of very little restriction. Infact, between gear changes, the turbine is still spinning much faster than the exhaust flowing around it. This helps to relieve backpressure when the engine speed drops. One last thing for the turbo, a well matched turbo can provide maximum boost through much of your RPM range. A supercharger will either reach max boost at max rpm only or it will vent the extra boost to prevent leaning out the motor. For the former, you are wasting potential power and for the later, you are using excess crank horsepower (because you still have to compress that extra air). <P>One point the supercharger guys will make (a very valid point) is that you have no lag. That really depends on how the supercharger is geared but it's a fact. But a well matched turbo system won't have a great deal of lag. In a street racing event, if you are cruising and someone pulls up next to you and you are running about 2grand with negitive manifold pressure, when you mash the throttle and downshift, you have to wait a split second for your turbo to spool (or several seconds if your turbo is poorly matched). A supercharged car will get what boost it is going to get right then and there. But over your run (several cycles of engine speed-up, up-shift, engine speed-up), depending how long your run is, you will be under more boost longer. <P>Superchargers are often much easier to install. A turbo has the luxury of being an independant unit and can be mounted anywhere convienent. Turbos can add a good deal of engine compartment heat because so much energy is used at the turbine and is converted to heat. All in all, on a fiero, I think turbos make great poweradders but a supercharged 3.8 is also pretty damn cool. So it's a matter of personal preference.<P>Hope this helps..<P>-Bryan<BR>||||reg||1
