Z||000009||RedOktober||09-21-2001||03:03 AM||||Turbo88"Ok this is a shameless plug . I am making a turbo kit for the 2.8lt engine. It should be done sometime next month. /plug"<P>Keep me posted on your kit.. I want another, but not going the Miller Woods Set up(aka Design 1) again.. IHI turbo too spendy to rebuild and is way too small for the 2.8/3.4.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red6[1]a.jpg">  <BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"||||reg||1
Z||000010||Predator||09-21-2001||10:36 AM||||I have to agree that the IHI turbo is expensive to rebuild, but from what I have been told it is a very well designed turbo. It is small, but packs a big punch. I run 9 lbs of boost without a problem. I have talked to others, and it really is not too small especially for the street. It spools up extremely fast so there is practically no lag, in fact since I got it I can't say what lag is since I can't detect a hesitation when I accelerate. I am by no means a turbo expert, and am going by my one turbo setup with advise from my mechanic who tunes turbos and superchargers for a living. He stated that the turbo I have is one of the best built you can get. That is why it is so expensive to rebuild. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||09-21-2001||10:53 AM||||My personal opinion is a turbo is superior to a supercharger from a performance and driveability standpoint (properly designed).  I had an 87 Grand National, and yes, it's huge turbo had some turbo lag, but that wasn't so bad.  It actually helped launch the car, so you didn't just blow the tires away.  I could dial in my launch by power braking up to a certain boost pressure, rather than judge it by rpms.  That allowed me to time the turbo lag to the limit of the tire's adhesion.  If I had time to properly stage the car - NOTHING in it's class could out launch me.  The only car I've had that was faster was my 72 Trans Am (455HO), but it was harder to launch.  If you didn't get it just right, that big block would just spin the tires all day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The main reason most manufacturers use superchargers instead of turbos is emissions.  Turbos absorb a lot of heat and it takes longer for the catalytic converter to "light off" and start functioning on a turbo car vs. a normally aspirated or supercharged one.  Most of the emissions emitted by cars these days come in those warm up minutes, so reducing that has the biggest impact on emissions.  A cat has to get hot before it starts working.<P>I'm considering a 3800SC swap mainly because it's a complete factory designed package-so it's got reliability and performance.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Turbo88||09-21-2001||02:14 PM||||I like turbo's for the above reasons. I think, centrifugal compressors (turbos and vortech style superchargers) are far more effiecent than roots type blowers. Roots blowers are probably the easiest to implement. As a point of fact, the 16-71 roots blowers you see on top fuel cars takes over 600hp to turn at full speed. That is around 10% of the engines total output being used by the blower alone. Now when are making 5000-6000hp, you have plenty to spare. <P>As far as my kit goes, the reason it is takign so long to develope is, the test car needed a new transaxle. Seems the pervious owner developed a leak and burned it up. The infamous getrag selector cable was also broken. We all know how fun getrag parts are to find! I also had to replace the steering rack. Try finding one of THOSE for an 88! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Anyway, I have collected a stack of parts I need to put the car back to running condition again. This kit will be based on a v6 5speed which seems to be the most popular configuration. I am also trying to buy an 87 GT automatic which will serve as a test bed for the automatic exhaust. Ultimately, I want to redesign the entire exhaust system and provide that system with the kit I am making. It will use the stock manifolds sinse those are such a pain to change but I will offer a balanced header upgrade if someone wants to go through the pain of installing them. <P>Eventually, I am going to offer a turbo motor upgrade kit that will allow to safely run much higher levels of boost without shelling the motor.<P>When it is completed and I am satisfied the kit is what it should be, I am planning a group purchase of kits. This should run some time in October or November. Probably the later because I want to test and retest and run the car for all it's worth without blowing it up. Once I find that happy median between good performance and good reliability, I'll post a group purchase and discount the price if I can sell enough of them. Anyway I'll keep everyone updated...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cooter||09-21-2001||03:40 PM||||I am going with a roots style blower on my 3.4, from a T-bird Supercoupe. The reason? Lower operating speed at the rotors, so less heat and friction. At the speeds that the M90 blower will be turning (less than 9000 rpms), it will take less than 30 horsepower to turn (according the the Eaton website)<BR>I have already had a custom exhaust system put on my car and I did not want to pay for another to use a turbo. I have a rebuildable IHI from a TurboCoupe I will sell for $10 plus shipping if somebody wants it.||||reg||1
Z||000014||MinnGreenGT||09-21-2001||04:12 PM||||Turbo88... do you have even a rough estimate for cost on your turbo kit? (BTW: I have an '87GT auto w/3.1L you can 'borrow' as a test bed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  I'd just like to know what I'll need to sell to get the cash (cat, Big-Screen, SUV, house...?)  Are we talking less than $1k, or like $3k, or ...?||||reg||1
Z||000015||pureSKID||11-07-2001||08:34 PM||||Turbo lag is not near as bad as people make it out to be.  For example... say you redline at 6500 rpm and build max turbo boost at 3500 rpm.  When you drag someone, you will never see below (guessing) 4000 or more rpms, so turbo lag doesn't even factor in at all.<P>On an autocross type race, a road race, or punching it while on say a 40mph role, yeah maybe your turbo lag will hurt a bit.. but remember, full power is a downshift away||||reg||1
Z||000016||Wannabe||11-07-2001||09:50 PM||||If you don't have turbo lag, then all you have is lag.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I like turbos - my Saab is a blast of a daily driver - but unless it's done by the factory or a very experienced tuner, cubes are ususally better.||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||11-07-2001||10:14 PM||||My IRM turbo comes up between 2000-2500 rpm, so there is no detectable lag. Keeps building clear up to redline past 7000 rpm.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||TRiAD||11-08-2001||01:29 AM||||There was another thread where Turbo88 and I debated this at length, try a search for it. <P>That said, most of your replies here are knowledgeable. I prefer SCs for the low-end torque, the bypass system incorporated to Eaton SCs, and reliability issues. <P>Both have their place, and I would take a turbo if you're looking for more than 10psi, or only looking for "straight line" performance. For me, SCs offer more "real world" performance; but I like to cook in the corners.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW, I'll shamelessly plug as well, over the winter, I will be developing an Eaton SC kit for the factory 2.8 V6, in 6 and 10psi trim levels, and am striving to keep the cost at $3K maximum. This will be complete, with gauges and fuel management, and will incluse a bypass, so the SC is not in use at less than 75% throttle. <P>Best of luck, you should have a blast, whichever way you go. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/85-Sig-2.gif">              <BR><B><I><u><FONT COLOR="red">TRiAD Motorsports</FONT></u></B> \\\\ <B>triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> //// Michael ~ 85 GT 4sp</I>||||reg||1
